1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,685
Open the C06 template.

2
00:00:01,685 --> 00:00:06,783
It is short on purpose: a list you can actually fill in during a coffee break, not a

3
00:00:06,783 --> 00:00:10,606
form that becomes its own chore and gets abandoned by week two.

4
00:00:10,606 --> 00:00:15,643
Fill it in for the first agent you built, Agent 1, before you move on, so the habit

5
00:00:15,643 --> 00:00:19,224
is real rather than theoretical the first time you need it.

6
00:00:19,224 --> 00:00:23,593
Why does this one need paper when the habit already exists in your head?

7
00:00:23,593 --> 00:00:25,475
Because trials are rare events.

8
00:00:25,475 --> 00:00:30,148
You will run one this month and perhaps not another for a quarter, and skills

9
00:00:30,148 --> 00:00:33,061
exercised quarterly do not stay sharp in memory.

10
00:00:33,061 --> 00:00:38,158
Paper also travels: when Dev picks up Priya's templates in Section 10, the checklist

11
00:00:38,158 --> 00:00:42,771
is what lets him run his own trial properly without Priya standing over him.

12
00:00:42,771 --> 00:00:44,106
The videos taught you.

13
00:00:44,106 --> 00:00:48,536
The artifact is what survives you, exactly as promised back in Section 0.

14
00:00:49,086 --> 00:00:52,409
Notice section three asks for a number, not a feeling.

15
00:00:52,409 --> 00:00:57,517
Fill it in before you start, the same way a real experiment sets its sample size in

16
00:00:57,517 --> 00:00:58,010
advance.

17
00:00:58,010 --> 00:01:02,933
Three to five runs is a reasonable default for a weekly agent, more for anything

18
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:04,841
that touches a client directly.

19
00:01:04,841 --> 00:01:07,980
Each section is aimed at a specific way trials rot.

20
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:13,150
Section one stops the trial that starts before there is anything to measure against:

21
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:18,011
no expected answer written down means every output gets graded on plausibility,

22
00:01:18,011 --> 00:01:20,473
which extraction taught you to distrust.

23
00:01:20,473 --> 00:01:25,458
Section two stops the comfortable sample, the human instinct to check the outputs

24
00:01:25,458 --> 00:01:29,336
that look interesting and wave through the ones that look fine.

25
00:01:29,336 --> 00:01:34,382
And section three's second pair of eyes is smaller than it sounds: not an approval

26
00:01:34,382 --> 00:01:39,552
board, just one colleague reading your trial log for ten minutes, because the person

27
00:01:39,552 --> 00:01:44,475
who built the agent is the one person guaranteed to read its results generously.

28
00:01:44,475 --> 00:01:48,968
Priya's second pair is whoever the job description's sign off line names.

29
00:01:48,968 --> 00:01:51,922
Yours can be anyone who did not build the thing.

30
00:01:52,472 --> 00:01:57,766
This is the same instinct behind Agent 4's draft-only build in Section 6, applied to

31
00:01:57,766 --> 00:02:00,666
a moment in time instead of a piece of wiring.

32
00:02:00,666 --> 00:02:05,708
Both exist because "it has worked so far" is never, on its own, a reason to stop

33
00:02:05,708 --> 00:02:06,275
checking.

34
00:02:06,275 --> 00:02:11,381
Watch the question produce different answers across your own four agents, because

35
00:02:11,381 --> 00:02:12,830
that is what it is for.

36
00:02:12,830 --> 00:02:17,936
Triage gone wrong unreviewed costs a flag you disagree with at tomorrow's coffee:

37
00:02:17,936 --> 00:02:21,843
low, which is why one clean week can genuinely end that trial.

38
00:02:21,843 --> 00:02:26,949
The chaser gone wrong unreviewed is words in front of a client wearing your name:

39
00:02:26,949 --> 00:02:32,180
high, which is why its trial reads every single draft and why the wiring beneath it

40
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:32,936
never sends.

41
00:02:32,936 --> 00:02:37,538
Same question, honestly answered twice, two different amounts of caution.

42
00:02:37,538 --> 00:02:41,193
The checklist is not there to make you careful everywhere.

43
00:02:41,193 --> 00:02:44,471
It is there to spend your care where the cost lives.

44
00:02:45,021 --> 00:02:49,693
Next lecture is where this checklist gets tested against a real story: a run that

45
00:02:49,693 --> 00:02:51,250
failed, and what caught it.
